The Statistical Standard on Economic Loss (GB 6721-86) [29] refers to all economic losses arising from accidents involving workers in the labour process, including direct and indirect economic losses.
Direct economic loss is the cost and value of the destruction of property resulting from the loss of life and injury caused by the accident, and after-care expenses, which are measured in the following terms:
(a) Costs incurred after personal injury or death: medical expenses (including nursing expenses), funeral and pension expenses, allowances and relief expenses, rest pay;
(a) Aftercare costs: transactional costs of handling accidents, on-site rescue costs, site clean-up costs, accident fines and compensation costs;
Value of property loss: value of loss of fixed assets, value of loss of current assets.
Indirect economic loss is the value of other losses caused by the loss of output, resource damage and the impact of the accident, which is statistically defined as:
(a) The value of the cut-off or reduced production loss;
